<h2>The allegation</h2>
<p>The complaint came from a Senior Accounts Officer at a central pay and accounts office under the Ministry of Home Affairs. A special audit had surfaced unauthorised &ldquo;Miscellaneous Dues&rdquo; entries in salary bills, quantifying the loss at about Rs 1,43,78,690. The applicant — a Multi-Tasking Staff employee — found about Rs 32.44 lakh credited to his salary account without any corresponding claim. The chargesheet alleged that the money travelled onward from his account to co-accused Anjani Kumar Singh, who withdrew it through self-cheques.</p>
<p>The applicant was arrested on 28 April 2026. His bail applications before the Magistrate and the Sessions Judge were dismissed on 20 May 2026 and 3 July 2026 respectively — both, notably, before the chargesheet was filed.</p>

<h2>The court&rsquo;s reasoning</h2>
<p>Three strands carried the order. First, role: the applicant had no part in preparing or approving the salary bills, and the court found his role &ldquo;extremely limited&rdquo; compared with the main co-accused (para 12). An MTS employee sits at the receiving end of the payroll system, not its controls. Second, the nature of the evidence: the case rests on documents already collected (para 13), so custody adds nothing to the investigation and the usual apprehensions of tampering carry little force. Third, antecedents and conduct: no previous involvement, and satisfactory jail conduct (para 14).</p>
<div class="note">
<p>The court invoked the principle that &ldquo;bail is rule and jail is the exception&rdquo; (para 10), citing <em>Manish Sisodia v. Directorate of Enforcement</em>, (2024) 12 SCC 660, which in turn draws on <em>Javed Gulam Nabi Shaikh v. State of Maharashtra</em>, (2024) 9 SCC 813. Significantly, the prosecution did not oppose bail.</p>
</div>

<h2>Conditions of release</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Personal bond of Rs 10,000 with one surety of the like amount.</li>
  <li>No departure from the NCT of Delhi without the trial court&rsquo;s permission.</li>
  <li>Monthly appearance before the Investigating Officer.</li>
  <li>Further conditions as recorded in the order, including the usual undertakings against tampering and fresh offences.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>What the order teaches about economic-offence bail</h2>
<p>Economic offences are routinely described as grave, and the headline loss figure — here, over a crore — can dominate the bail hearing. This order is a reminder that gravity is assessed person by person, not file by file. The questions that decided the matter were granular: who keyed in the entries, who approved the bills, whose signature moved the money, and what remains to be investigated. A beneficiary account holder whose role ends at receipt stands on a different footing from the official who operated the system.</p>
<p>For the defence, the practical lessons are to place the chargesheet&rsquo;s own role attribution before the court, to emphasise completed investigation and documentary evidence, and to document custody conduct. For complainant departments, the order underlines that recovery of defrauded amounts is pursued through the trial and attachment mechanisms — prolonged pre-trial custody of a peripheral accused is not the instrument for it.</p>
